\f4\fs26 It had only taken him a few days to organise the patches, all his old contacts still in business, were obliging and well stocked. The biggest issue arose when Cracker tried to arrange a work visa for the Health, Respite and Retreat Zone (HRRZ), which took another 7 days of submitting forms, arguing, negotiating and bullshitting Quarantine and Immigration Officials. \
The HRRZ, a Level 10 Security area was highly restricted, existing solely for the purpose of pampering the imagined ailments of the ultra-rich. Only those workers absolutely necessary to meet the needs of the well-heeled clients were allowed the honour of breathing the same air as the chosen few. That meant a bloated workforce replete with over-paid sycophants and underpaid slaves. There were so many dirty working class drones, in fact, that they required their own medical centre, the very facility Cracker and Sarah were to meet.\
Foremost on Cracker\'92s mind was, against all odds, impressing Sarah when he met up with her again. Acting cool was not something he had ever been able to pull off, falling painfully short of the mark each occasion he had made the attempt. Pretending to be someone else did not come easily to him and the fa\'e7ade could only be sustained for a short time before eventually trapping himself in a nonsensical network of deception. In all but exceptional cases, he cared not what others thought of him, but Sarah was most exceptional and he did not want to look the fool. It would be the worst of all fates to be dismissed by her as a joke and although he would not openly admit it, his most dreadful fear was to be laughed at by the woman he loved. The shock of being cut down so easily by a mere giggle and being left so deflated, triggered within him an unconscious primeval explosion of rage, erupting from somewhere deep in his caveman brain. His validity as a man rejected in its entirety, or so it felt. He had always managed to swallowed it down long enough for more coherent thought to return, but was ashamed something so innocuous could inspire such primordial anger. \
 \
Cracker would be nothing but polite and obliging as possible, holding out no hope she would ever even like him. He would not tell jokes and he would keep sexual innuendo to a bare minimum, exposing Sarah to the full brunt of his banality. With no expectations he would have no disappointments and when he finally got her, he thought, it would be even sweeter. \
Sarah did not know why they had to meet at the seedy rear of the neglected medical centre where, she feared, they threw out bio-hazard waste and hacked off limbs. \'93What are we doing here? I put the security cameras are on a loop, there is no chance of being recorded.\'94\
\'93This is where we are going.\'94 Cracker pointed to a faded sign, hanging 30 degrees or more out of level, bearing one word \'96 Morgue. \
\'93The morgue? This is where we get our tests?\'94 This made Sarah nauseous, even more so than she had been over the past couple of weeks.\
\'93They\'92ve got the best resources in the entire public hospital system. C\'92mon, I\'92ll introduce you to Dr Logan. You are in for a treat. She is the second smartest person I have ever met.\'94\
\'93Second, huh? Sounds impressive.\'94 Sarah replied, assuming she was the smartest person he had ever met.\
They entered through unlocked security doors and were immediately hit with the overpowering stench of industrial grade disinfectant that and scorched their nostrils, causing Sarah\'92s stomach to spasm as she tried to hold down her dinner. \
\'93Cracker!\'94 Yelled the good Dr Logan from behind a dirty, cluttered reception desk. She hurdled the desk and hugged Cracker like a much loved brother.\
\'93Kathy, it\'92s so good to see you. How long has it been?\'94 Cracker grinned, only now recalling the delight of her company.\
\'93About 4 years I think. I\'92m not working the wards now. They decided working on the dead was the best option for me. Just as long as everything gets done, they let me do what I like.\'94 \
\'93And I know what you like!\'94 Cracker broke out a box full of patches, some old favourites and some very new experiments. \
\'93Wow, Surprise Party. I love these, they are so unpredictable. Thank you.\'94 It was well after hours and well past the time to patch-up again. \
Cracker first bumped into his contact in the world of pathology when he was contracted for regular maintenance in the public medical centres of The Pale. The first year intern had knocked off for the day, stumbled out of the staff exit and staggered off into the night. Hours later they crossed paths again in the quarter when he spied her, arms littered with sticky and variously coloured patches, dancing solo in the street. She had been hyper-patched by a crew of scoundrels and by that time Cracker found her, she was completely incoherent and unable, even, to remove the patches. Her pre-rape stagger saw Kathy a prime candidate for abuse and although his natural reaction should have been leave her to her fate, Cracker adopted the role of protector and managed to fight off a few would-be assailants to get her back to the hospital dorm intact. For the next couple of years, he would fetch patches for her, limiting the good doctor\'92s exposure to the vultures on the street, but had lost contact when she was transferred to the Health, Respite and Retreat Zone.\
Patches were perfect recreation for the self-medicating urban professional. Hallucinogens, stimulants, depressants, performance enhancers and anything else medical miscreants can cook up were infused into patches, adhered to the upper arm and a party ensues. Some patches were medication masala, a combination of many flavours, but Kathy\'92s favourite was \'93Organised Party\'94. You could plan the perfect night out with the release of different drugs at specific times, seamlessly slipping from one buzz to the other or into periods of absolute clarity then plunging into psychedelic hilarity. Unless you had been the victim of hyper-patching, the effects begin to wear off directly upon removal, but Kathy patched all day, everyday, her work schedule neatly wedged in between periods of incoherence of one type or another. She rolled up her sleeve, closed her eyes and fished her hand around in the box, picking a patch at random.\
\'93Wait a minute. Can we get to why we are here?\'94 Sarah wanted to leave the morgue as soon as possible. \'93Sarah Jacobson, PhD, L.5.\'94 She offered her hand and they shook in business-like fashion.\
\'93Kathy Logan, MBBS, L.5. Is this your girlfriend, Cracker?\'94 She asked with a tone of suspicion. 
\f5\i This L.5 is stunning and Cracker is, average at best, but I must admit he is looking healthier than ever. How on earth did he make friends with her?
\f4\i0 \
\'93No.\'94 Sarah pursed her lips into a cats-bum configuration. \
\'93Sounds unequivocal. I was going to say she is a bit out of your league, young man. How did you two meet?\'94\
\'93We met at work.\'94 Cracker said truthfully.\
\'93Cleaning out her office I bet. Ha, Ha.\'94 Even though she was not patching at that point in time, she still giggled after everything said.\
\'93Yep.\'94 Cracker admitted as he shuffled his feet.\
\'93So from what Cracker tells me you want routine bloods and scans? What am I looking for?\
\'93We were exposed to a bio-agent. We don\'92t know what it is.\'94 Sarah decided to be short and to the point. Brevity would speed up the process.\
\'93Well, that was a bit silly, wasn\'92t it? Do you have a sample?\'94 Kathy was hopeful. It makes it a hell of a lot easier when you know what to look for.\
\'93Sorry.\'94 Cracker silently scolded himself. He should have thought of getting a sample.\
\'93Pathology blindfolded, my favourite.\'94 Kathy laughed. \'93I am not a microbiologist and these machines only test for known human pathogens. Foreign bio-matter is sometimes easy to chase down, but other times it takes quite a while to ID.\'94\
Cracker had been correct about the facility, the building was be falling down around them, but the testing and scanning equipment were state of the art and all in house. Dr Logan took samples of tissue and blood from all parts of their bodies. They were x-rayed, ultra-sounded, full body scanned and poked at a bit.\
\'93The results should not take too long. Do you want a coffee or something, I am going to hit a patch for about half an hour.\'94 A patch slapped into her upper arm.\
\'93What about billing?\'94 Sarah did not want her name appearing anywhere in the records.\
\'93Not a problem at the moment. I will use false idents and bill to an old case number. Good old Emil Kalheri.\'94 \
\'93Emil Kalheri?\'94 Cracker asked and stared in open-mouthed amazement at Sarah. \
\'93How do you know of Emil Kalheri?\'94 Sarah asked, hoping Kathy was still able to reply. \
\'93He had died of cancer and was brought in by a coronial meat-wagon, dropped in the street according to the report. There was a lot of undue interest in such a mundane case. Company agents and police asking questions, demanding to look at the body and the next day a woman posing as his wife signed him out. The police ordered me to keep his account open. So I can bill what I like to it.\'94 She slipped into incoherence and curled up on the floor. \
Precisely half an hour had passed and Dr Logan burst back in to life, her mind perfectly clear. \'93Well, I\'92ve got 40 minutes of lucidity so let\'92s look at the results.\'94\
\'93Apart from slightly raised body temperatures, both of you are exactly 0.15 degrees above normal, you are in remarkable health. (Rattle off usual results \'96 liver function, etc) DNA sequences clean, no sign of any foreign matter in your blood, no known viruses or bacteria and toxicology clear. Cracker, I am impressed. When did you give up the gear?\'94\
\'93I didn\'92t as far as I know.\'94 Cracker was as surprised as the good doctor.\
\'93Sarah, there was a result with the scans that I wanted to confirm with the blood tests. Oh yes! It is odd because my examination indicated you are 
\f5\i virgo intacto
\f4\i0 , but congratulations you guys! You are pregnant!\'94 She went to hug Sarah but was pushed aside.\
